setRequiredLicense [SimTalk]
Tells the library folder designated by <Folder-Path> to request a user-defined license.
Remarks
When you load a simulation model or an object, which contains such a library, Plant Simulation looks for
the corresponding user-defined license in the Windows Registry. If it does not find a valid license with the
specified feature name, Plant Simulation cannot load the simulation model or the object respectively.
The registered license is only valid when the version is greater than or equal to the requested version,
when the Host-ID of the license matches the Host-ID of the computer and when the validity date of the
license has not been exceeded, and when the password of the registered license matches the password of
the requested license.
By using a user-defined license you can restrict the passing on of simulation models and objects, including
libraries.
• To restrict the passing on of a simulation model, call basis.setRequiredLicense and then save the
model.
• To restrict the passing on of a library, call setRequiredLicense for the library folder and then save
the library.
When you update a library that requires a user-defined library, the new version of the library also has to
request this user-defined license (i.e., the same feature), and the requested version number has to be as
least as great as the old library, and the license has to be protected with the same password.
To assign a different password to the newer version of the library, call the method setRequiredLicense and
specify an empty string "" for the parameter Feature and the old password as the parameter Password.
This de-registers the user-defined license. Then call the method setRequiredLicense again and specify the
name of the license feature to the parameter Feature and the new password to the parameter Password,
and the old password to the parameter PreviousPasswordForUpdate. The library can then not only update
versions of the library that use the new password according to the rules described above, but also any
version of the library that use the old password, even then when the name of the license feature changes
or the new version number is smaller than the version number of the old library.
Type
Method
Syntax
<Folder-Path>.setRequiredLicense(Feature:string, Version:string, 
Password:string[, FreeInLicense:string, Comment:string, 
PreviousPasswordForUpdate:string])
setRequiredLicense [SimTalk]
9-1196 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Feature of data type string designates the name of the license feature. Specify an empty
string "" to remove an existing license provided you specify the correct password.
• The parameter Version of data type string designates the version number of the license. The registered
version has to be greater than or equal to the requested license.
If, for example, version 10 of the feature is registered, you can request version 9.5.0 but not vice versa.
• The parameter Password of data type string designates a secret password. The password of the
registered license has to match the this password so that the registered license is considered valid.
• The optional parameter FreeInLicense of data type string sets that the user-defined license is not
requested when Plant Simulation uses the requested license type or a license type of lower value. You
can enter "Application", "Educational", "Runtime", "Simulation", "", or "-".
The values have the following meaning:
"-": The license is never free, not even in the Viewer license.
"": The license is only free in the Viewer license. This is the default setting when you do not enter the
parameter.
"Simulation": The license is free in the Simulation and in the lower Viewer license.
"Runtime": The license is free in the Runtime license and in the lower license types, i.e., Simulation
and Viewer.
"Application": The license is free in the Application license and in the lower license types, i.e.,.
Runtime, Simulation, and Viewer.
"Educational": The license is free in the Educational license and in the lower license types, i.e.,.
Runtime, Simulation, and Viewer.
• The optional parameter Comment of data type string sets the text which will be attached to the
message, which is shown in the message window when no license exists when loading the object or the
model. This only applies if you did enter any text.
• The optional parameter PreviousPasswordForUpdate of data type string designates the previous
password for updating the library.
You can generate user-defined licenses with the SimTalk function createLicenseFile.
Example
.Standard.MyLibrary.setRequiredLicense("MyFeature", "9.5.7", 
"MySecretPassword123")
setRequiredLicense [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 9-1197
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

